-- title:
--   Piecewise integral projective homeomorphisms of the simplex, the interval and the circle
-- statement:
--   Cannon–Floyd–Parry §7 (pp. 249–254): the definitions behind W. Thurston's piecewise integral projective models of $F$ and $T$.
--
--   **Simplex, ρ.** `Simplex n` is $\Delta_n$, the points of $\mathbb{R}^{n+1}$ with nonnegative coordinates summing to $1$ (Mathlib's `stdSimplex`). `rho x` is $x / \sum_i |x_i|$ (p. 249). `glAct A x` is the integer matrix $A \in GL(n+1, \mathbb{Z})$ applied to $x$.
--
--   **Integral projective maps.** A map $f \colon \Delta_n \to \Delta_n$ is integral projective on $U$ via $A$ (`IsIntegralProjectiveVia`) when for every $x \in U$, $Ax$ has nonnegative coordinates and $f(x) = \rho(Ax)$; `IsIntegralProjective` asks for some such $A$ (p. 249). Only the values on $U$ matter.
--
--   **Subsimplices and subdivisions.** A rational point of $\Delta_n$ has rational coordinates; a rational subsimplex is a set of affinely independent rational points of $\Delta_n$ (its vertices). An integral subsimplex (`IsIntegralSubsimplex n S`) is a set $S$ that is the image of $\Delta_n$ under a map integral projective on all of $\Delta_n$ (p. 249). A subdivision of $\Delta_n$ (`IsSubdivision`) is a finite geometric simplicial complex (Mathlib's `Geometry.SimplicialComplex`) with underlying space $\Delta_n$; it is rational, or integral, when every face with $n+1$ vertices is a rational subsimplex, or has integral convex hull. `Refines K' K` says $K'$ and $K$ have the same space and each face of $K'$ lies in the convex hull of a face of $K$.
--
--   **Lift and index (p. 250).** `lift x` is the integer vector $w$ with nonnegative coordinates, $\rho(w) = x$ and coordinates of greatest common divisor $1$ (chosen when one exists, $0$ otherwise). `ind v` is the index in $\mathbb{Z}^{n+1}$ of the subgroup generated by the lifts of $v_0, \dots, v_n$ ($0$ if infinite).
--
--   **PIP maps.** A homeomorphism $f$ of $\Delta_n$ is PIP (`IsPIP`) when some integral subdivision has $f$ integral projective on (the convex hull of) each face; `PIPSet n` is the set of these. `IsPIPPlus`/`PIPPlusSet n` ask moreover that each face's matrix have determinant $1$: this is how orientation-preserving (p. 251) is expressed. `toSimplexOne t` is the point $(t, 1-t)$ of $\Delta_1$.
--
--   **The interval (pp. 251–253).** On $[0,1]$, identified with $\Delta_1' = \{(t,1)\}$, `IsIntegralProjective01 U f` says $U \subseteq [0,1]$ and for some $A \in GL(2,\mathbb{Z})$ and all $t \in U$, $A(t,1) = (x, y)$ with $0 \le x \le y$ and $f(t) = x/y$. `IsIntegralSubsimplex01 p q` says $[p, q]$ is the image of $[0,1]$ under such a map. `FracInterval` records $[a/b, c/d]$ by four natural numbers, with `lo`, `hi` its endpoints, `leftPart` $= [a/b, (a+c)/(b+d)]$, `rightPart` $= [(a+c)/(b+d), c/d]$, and `IsFarey` the conditions of p. 251: $b, c, d > 0$, $a \le b$, $c \le d$, $ad - bc = -1$. `fareyNode w` follows the path $w$ from $[0/1, 1/1]$, `false` taking the left part and `true` the right part.
--
--   **PIP⁺ of [0,1], tree diagrams.** `IsIntegralPartition01` is a list $0 = x_0, \dots, x_k = 1$ with each consecutive pair an integral subsimplex. `IsPIPPlus01 f`, for an order isomorphism $f$ of $[0,1]$, asks for such a partition with $f$ integral projective on each interval; `PIPPlus01Set` collects them. An order isomorphism is an orientation-preserving homeomorphism, so no determinant condition is added. `fareyMarks t` is the partition of $[0,1]$ cut out by the leaves of a tree read on $\mathcal{T}'$ (splitting at mediants), and `RepresentsPIP d f` says $f$ is integral projective on each interval of the domain tree's partition and carries its breakpoints in order to the range tree's.
--
--   **PIP⁺ of the circle.** `IsPIPPlusCircle f`, for a permutation of `UnitAddCircle`, asks for an order isomorphism $L$ of $\mathbb{R}$ with $L(x+1) = L(x) + 1$ covering $f$, and an integral partition of $[0,1]$ on each interval of which $L$ minus some integer is integral projective; `PIPPlusCircleSet` collects them. As for $T$, the order-preserving lift is what makes the map orientation-preserving.
--
--   **pipA, pipB, pipC.** The three piecewise linear fractional functions of p. 254, defined on $\mathbb{R}$ by their formulas on $[0,\tfrac12]$, $[\tfrac12,\tfrac23]$, … as printed.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Products of homeomorphisms and permutations compose right to left. Division by zero is $0$ in Lean; it never arises for the maps above.
-- source:
--   Cannon, J. W., Floyd, W. J., Parry, W. R., Introductory notes on Richard Thompson's groups, L'Enseignement Mathématique (2) 42 (1996) 215–256, https://doi.org/10.5169/seals-87877, section 7, pp. 249–254, Δₙ, ρ, integral projective maps, rational and integral subdivisions, PIP(Δₙ) (p. 249), the index (p. 250), PIP⁺(Δₙ), Δ₁′, integral subsimplices of [0,1] and the tree 𝒯′ (p. 251), PIP⁺(S¹) (p. 253), the maps of p. 254

import Definitions.Def_CannonFloydParry
import Definitions.Def_CannonFloydParry_Trees
import Definitions.Def_CannonFloydParry_TreeDiagrams
import Mathlib

/-!
# Piecewise integral projective homeomorphisms

Cannon–Floyd–Parry, *Introductory notes on Richard Thompson's groups*,
L'Enseignement Mathématique (2) **42** (1996), 215–256, §7 (pages 249–254).

The simplex `Δₙ`, the projection `ρ`, integral projective maps, rational and integral
subsimplices and subdivisions, the index of a rational simplex, and the sets `PIP(Δₙ)` and
`PIP⁺(Δₙ)` (pp. 249–251); integral projective maps, integral subsimplices and the tree of
integral subsimplices of `[0,1]` (pp. 251–252); `PIP⁺` of `[0,1]` and of the circle, and the
tree diagrams read on the Farey tree (p. 253); the three maps of p. 254.
-/

namespace CannonFloydParry

/-! ### The simplex and integral projective maps (p. 249) -/

/-- The `n`-simplex `Δₙ`: the points of `ℝ^{n+1}` with nonnegative coordinates summing to `1`. -/
abbrev Simplex (n : ℕ) : Set (Fin (n + 1) → ℝ) := stdSimplex ℝ (Fin (n + 1))

/-- `ρ(x) = x / |x|` with `|x| = ∑ |xᵢ|`. -/
noncomputable def rho {n : ℕ} (x : Fin (n + 1) → ℝ) : Fin (n + 1) → ℝ :=
  (∑ i, |x i|)⁻¹ • x

/-- An integer matrix acting on `ℝ^{n+1}`. -/
noncomputable def glAct {n : ℕ} (A : GL (Fin (n + 1)) ℤ) (x : Fin (n + 1) → ℝ) :
    Fin (n + 1) → ℝ :=
  Matrix.mulVec ((A : Matrix (Fin (n + 1)) (Fin (n + 1)) ℤ).map (Int.cast : ℤ → ℝ)) x

/-- `f` is integral projective on `U ⊆ Δₙ` by means of the matrix `A`: for `x ∈ U`, `A x` has
nonnegative coordinates and `f x = ρ (A x)`. -/
def IsIntegralProjectiveVia {n : ℕ} (A : GL (Fin (n + 1)) ℤ) (U : Set (Simplex n))
    (f : Simplex n → Simplex n) : Prop :=
  ∀ x ∈ U, (∀ i, 0 ≤ glAct A x i) ∧ (f x : Fin (n + 1) → ℝ) = rho (glAct A x)

/-- `f` is **integral projective** on `U ⊆ Δₙ` (p. 249). -/
def IsIntegralProjective {n : ℕ} (U : Set (Simplex n)) (f : Simplex n → Simplex n) : Prop :=
  ∃ A : GL (Fin (n + 1)) ℤ, IsIntegralProjectiveVia A U f

/-! ### Rational and integral subsimplices and subdivisions (p. 249) -/

/-- A **rational point** of `Δₙ`: a point of `Δₙ` with rational coordinates. -/
def IsRationalPoint {n : ℕ} (x : Fin (n + 1) → ℝ) : Prop :=
  x ∈ Simplex n ∧ ∀ i, ∃ q : ℚ, x i = q

/-- A **rational subsimplex** of `Δₙ`, given by its vertex set: affinely independent rational
points of `Δₙ`. -/
def IsRationalSubsimplex {n : ℕ} (s : Finset (Fin (n + 1) → ℝ)) : Prop :=
  (∀ v ∈ s, IsRationalPoint v) ∧ AffineIndependent ℝ ((↑) : s → Fin (n + 1) → ℝ)

/-- An **integral subsimplex** of `Δₙ`: the image of `Δₙ` under a map that is integral
projective on all of `Δₙ` (p. 249). -/
def IsIntegralSubsimplex (n : ℕ) (S : Set (Fin (n + 1) → ℝ)) : Prop :=
  ∃ f : Simplex n → Simplex n, IsIntegralProjective Set.univ f ∧
    Set.range (fun x => (f x : Fin (n + 1) → ℝ)) = S

/-- A **simplicial subdivision** of `Δₙ`: a finite geometric simplicial complex whose underlying
space is `Δₙ`. -/
def IsSubdivision (n : ℕ) (K : Geometry.SimplicialComplex ℝ (Fin (n + 1) → ℝ)) : Prop :=
  K.faces.Finite ∧ K.space = Simplex n

/-- A **rational subdivision** of `Δₙ`: every `n`-simplex (face with `n + 1` vertices) is a
rational subsimplex (p. 249). -/
def IsRationalSubdivision (n : ℕ) (K : Geometry.SimplicialComplex ℝ (Fin (n + 1) → ℝ)) :
    Prop :=
  IsSubdivision n K ∧ ∀ s ∈ K.faces, s.card = n + 1 → IsRationalSubsimplex s

/-- An **integral subdivision** of `Δₙ`: every `n`-simplex is an integral subsimplex (p. 249). -/
def IsIntegralSubdivision (n : ℕ) (K : Geometry.SimplicialComplex ℝ (Fin (n + 1) → ℝ)) :
    Prop :=
  IsSubdivision n K ∧ ∀ s ∈ K.faces, s.card = n + 1 → IsIntegralSubsimplex n (convexHull ℝ ↑s)

/-- `K'` **refines** `K`: the same underlying space, and every simplex of `K'` lies in a simplex
of `K`. -/
def Refines {n : ℕ} (K' K : Geometry.SimplicialComplex ℝ (Fin (n + 1) → ℝ)) : Prop :=
  K'.space = K.space ∧
    ∀ s ∈ K'.faces, ∃ t ∈ K.faces, convexHull ℝ (↑s : Set (Fin (n + 1) → ℝ)) ⊆ convexHull ℝ ↑t

/-! ### The index of a rational simplex (p. 250) -/

/-- The **lift** of a rational point `x` of `Δₙ` (p. 249): the point `w` of `ℤ^{n+1}` with
nonnegative coordinates, `ρ(w) = x`, and coordinates of greatest common divisor `1`; `0` when
there is none. -/
noncomputable def lift {n : ℕ} (x : Fin (n + 1) → ℝ) : Fin (n + 1) → ℤ :=
  open Classical in
  if h : ∃ w : Fin (n + 1) → ℤ,
      (∀ i, 0 ≤ w i) ∧ rho (fun i => (w i : ℝ)) = x ∧ Finset.univ.gcd w = 1 then
    h.choose
  else 0

/-- The **index** of the rational `n`-simplex with vertices `v 0, …, v n` (p. 250): the index in
`ℤ^{n+1}` of the subgroup generated by the lifts of the vertices (`0` if it is infinite). -/
noncomputable def ind {n : ℕ} (v : Fin (n + 1) → Fin (n + 1) → ℝ) : ℕ :=
  (AddSubgroup.closure (Set.range fun j => lift (v j))).index

/-! ### PIP homeomorphisms of `Δₙ` (pp. 249, 251) -/

/-- A **piecewise integral projective** homeomorphism of `Δₙ` (p. 249): for some integral
subdivision, the restriction to each simplex is integral projective. -/
def IsPIP {n : ℕ} (f : Simplex n ≃ₜ Simplex n) : Prop :=
  ∃ S, IsIntegralSubdivision n S ∧
    ∀ σ ∈ S.faces, IsIntegralProjective {x : Simplex n | (x : Fin (n + 1) → ℝ) ∈ convexHull ℝ ↑σ} f

/-- `PIP(Δₙ)`, as a set of homeomorphisms. -/
def PIPSet (n : ℕ) : Set (Simplex n ≃ₜ Simplex n) := {f | IsPIP f}

/-- An **orientation-preserving** PIP homeomorphism of `Δₙ` (p. 251), expressed through the
pieces: for some integral subdivision, on each simplex the map is `ρ ∘ A` for a matrix `A` of
determinant `1`. -/
def IsPIPPlus {n : ℕ} (f : Simplex n ≃ₜ Simplex n) : Prop :=
  ∃ S, IsIntegralSubdivision n S ∧
    ∀ σ ∈ S.faces, ∃ A : GL (Fin (n + 1)) ℤ, (A : Matrix (Fin (n + 1)) (Fin (n + 1)) ℤ).det = 1 ∧
      IsIntegralProjectiveVia A {x : Simplex n | (x : Fin (n + 1) → ℝ) ∈ convexHull ℝ ↑σ} f

/-- `PIP⁺(Δₙ)`, as a set of homeomorphisms. -/
def PIPPlusSet (n : ℕ) : Set (Simplex n ≃ₜ Simplex n) := {f | IsPIPPlus f}

/-- The point `(t, 1 - t)` of `Δ₁`. -/
def toSimplexOne (t : UI) : Simplex 1 :=
  ⟨![(t : ℝ), 1 - t], by
    refine ⟨fun i => ?_, ?_⟩
    · fin_cases i
      · exact t.2.1
      · show (0 : ℝ) ≤ 1 - t
        linarith [t.2.2]
    · simp⟩

/-! ### `[0,1]` in the coordinates of `Δ₁′` (pp. 251–253) -/

/-- `f` is integral projective on `U` by means of `A ∈ GL(2, ℤ)`, in the coordinate `t` of
`[0,1]`, identified with `Δ₁′ = {(t, 1)}` (p. 251): for `t ∈ U`, `A (t, 1) = (x, y)` with
`0 ≤ x ≤ y`, and `f t = x / y`. -/
def IsIntegralProjective01Via (A : GL (Fin 2) ℤ) (U : Set ℝ) (f : ℝ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ t ∈ U, 0 ≤ glAct A ![t, 1] 0 ∧ glAct A ![t, 1] 0 ≤ glAct A ![t, 1] 1 ∧
    f t = glAct A ![t, 1] 0 / glAct A ![t, 1] 1

/-- `f` is **integral projective** on `U ⊆ [0,1]` (pp. 251–252). -/
def IsIntegralProjective01 (U : Set ℝ) (f : ℝ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  U ⊆ Set.Icc 0 1 ∧ ∃ A : GL (Fin 2) ℤ, IsIntegralProjective01Via A U f

/-- `[p, q]` is an **integral subsimplex** of `[0,1]` (p. 251): the image of `[0,1]` under a map
integral projective on `[0,1]`. -/
def IsIntegralSubsimplex01 (p q : ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ f : ℝ → ℝ, IsIntegralProjective01 (Set.Icc 0 1) f ∧ f '' Set.Icc 0 1 = Set.Icc p q

/-- An interval `[a/b, c/d]` recorded by the four natural numbers `a, b, c, d`. -/
structure FracInterval where
  a : ℕ
  b : ℕ
  c : ℕ
  d : ℕ
  deriving DecidableEq, Repr

namespace FracInterval

/-- The left endpoint `a/b`. -/
noncomputable def lo (I : FracInterval) : ℝ := (I.a : ℝ) / I.b

/-- The right endpoint `c/d`. -/
noncomputable def hi (I : FracInterval) : ℝ := (I.c : ℝ) / I.d

/-- The **left part** of `[a/b, c/d]`: `[a/b, (a+c)/(b+d)]` (p. 251). -/
def leftPart (I : FracInterval) : FracInterval := ⟨I.a, I.b, I.a + I.c, I.b + I.d⟩

/-- The **right part** of `[a/b, c/d]`: `[(a+c)/(b+d), c/d]` (p. 251). -/
def rightPart (I : FracInterval) : FracInterval := ⟨I.a + I.c, I.b + I.d, I.c, I.d⟩

/-- The conditions of p. 251 on `[a/b, c/d]`: `b`, `c`, `d` positive, `a ≤ b`, `c ≤ d`, and
`ad - bc = -1`. -/
def IsFarey (I : FracInterval) : Prop :=
  0 < I.b ∧ 0 < I.c ∧ 0 < I.d ∧ I.a ≤ I.b ∧ I.c ≤ I.d ∧ (I.a : ℤ) * I.d - (I.b : ℤ) * I.c = -1

end FracInterval

/-- The vertex of the tree `𝒯′` of integral subsimplices reached from the root `[0/1, 1/1]` by
the path `w`, read from the root: `false` takes the left part, `true` the right part. -/
def fareyNode (w : List Bool) : FracInterval :=
  w.foldl (fun I b => if b then I.rightPart else I.leftPart) ⟨0, 1, 1, 1⟩

/-! ### `PIP⁺` of `[0,1]` and tree diagrams on the Farey tree (p. 253) -/

/-- An **integral partition** of `[0,1]`: breakpoints `0 = x₀, …, xₙ = 1`, each consecutive pair
bounding an integral subsimplex. -/
def IsIntegralPartition01 (xs : List ℝ) : Prop :=
  xs.head? = some 0 ∧ xs.getLast? = some 1 ∧ xs.IsChain IsIntegralSubsimplex01

/-- An orientation-preserving PIP homeomorphism of `[0,1]` (pp. 251–253): an order isomorphism
of `[0,1]` that is integral projective on each interval of some integral partition. -/
def IsPIPPlus01 (f : UI ≃o UI) : Prop :=
  ∃ xs, IsIntegralPartition01 xs ∧
    xs.IsChain (fun p q => IsIntegralProjective01 (Set.Icc p q) (extend f))

/-- `PIP⁺` of `[0,1]`, as a set of order isomorphisms. -/
def PIPPlus01Set : Set (UI ≃o UI) := {f | IsPIPPlus01 f}

/-- The interior breakpoints of the partition of `I` cut out by the leaves of a tree, splitting
each interval at the Farey mediant. -/
def fareyMarksAux : TTree → FracInterval → List FracInterval
  | .leaf, _ => []
  | .node l r, I => fareyMarksAux l I.leftPart ++ I.rightPart :: fareyMarksAux r I.rightPart

/-- The partition of `[0,1]` cut out by the leaves of a tree read on `𝒯′`, as the increasing
list of its breakpoints. -/
noncomputable def fareyMarks (t : TTree) : List ℝ :=
  (0 : ℝ) :: ((fareyMarksAux t ⟨0, 1, 1, 1⟩).map FracInterval.lo ++ [1])

/-- `f` is the map of the tree diagram `d` read on `𝒯′` (p. 253): integral projective on each
interval cut out by the domain tree, carrying those breakpoints in order to the breakpoints cut
out by the range tree. -/
def RepresentsPIP (d : TreeDiagram) (f : UI ≃o UI) : Prop :=
  (fareyMarks d.dom).IsChain (fun p q => IsIntegralProjective01 (Set.Icc p q) (extend f)) ∧
    (fareyMarks d.dom).map (extend f) = fareyMarks d.ran

/-! ### `PIP⁺` of the circle (p. 253) and the maps of p. 254 -/

/-- An orientation-preserving PIP homeomorphism of `S¹ = ℝ/ℤ` (p. 253): a permutation covered by
an order isomorphism `L` of `ℝ` commuting with translation by `1`, such that on each interval of
some integral partition of `[0,1]`, `L` minus an integer is integral projective. -/
def IsPIPPlusCircle (f : Equiv.Perm UnitAddCircle) : Prop :=
  ∃ L : ℝ ≃o ℝ, (∀ x, L (x + 1) = L x + 1) ∧
    (∀ x : ℝ, f (x : UnitAddCircle) = ((L x : ℝ) : UnitAddCircle)) ∧
    ∃ xs, IsIntegralPartition01 xs ∧
      xs.IsChain (fun p q => ∃ k : ℤ, IsIntegralProjective01 (Set.Icc p q) (fun t => L t - k))

/-- `PIP⁺(S¹)`, as a set of permutations of the circle. -/
def PIPPlusCircleSet : Set (Equiv.Perm UnitAddCircle) := {f | IsPIPPlusCircle f}

/-- The function of p. 254 corresponding to `A`. -/
noncomputable def pipA (t : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  if t ≤ 1 / 2 then t / (t + 1) else if t ≤ 2 / 3 then (-t + 1) / (-5 * t + 4) else (2 * t - 1) / t

/-- The function of p. 254 corresponding to `B`. -/
noncomputable def pipB (t : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  if t ≤ 1 / 2 then t
  else if t ≤ 2 / 3 then (3 * t - 1) / (4 * t - 1)
  else if t ≤ 3 / 4 then (-6 * t + 5) / (-11 * t + 9) else (2 * t - 1) / t

/-- The function of p. 254 corresponding to `C`. -/
noncomputable def pipC (t : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  if t ≤ 1 / 2 then (-3 * t + 2) / (-5 * t + 3)
  else if t ≤ 2 / 3 then (2 * t - 1) / t else (5 * t - 3) / (7 * t - 4)

end CannonFloydParry


